@inject Microsoft.AspNetCore.Antiforgery.IAntiforgery Antiforgery
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Toasts, straight from C#";
    Antiforgery.GetAndStoreTokens(Context);
}

<main>
    @* ---------------------------------------------------------------- HERO *@
    <section class="container hero">
        <div class="hero-copy">
            <span class="tag"><b>v2.0</b> Works with .NET 8 and .NET 10</span>
            <h1>Toast notifications for ASP.NET Core, <span class="accent">straight from C#</span></h1>
            <p class="lede">
                I built this package so you can show a toast with one line of C#. Call <code>notyf.Success("Saved!")</code>
                in your controller and the toast shows up on the page. It works after a redirect, after a form post,
                and on <code>fetch</code>, jQuery and htmx calls. You don't need jQuery, and there's no inline JavaScript.
            </p>
            <div class="hero-cta">
                <div class="install">
                    <span class="prompt">$</span>
                    <code id="code-hero-install">dotnet add package AspNetCoreHero.ToastNotification</code>
                    <button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="code-hero-install">copy</button>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="hero-cta hero-buttons">
                <a class="btn btn-primary" href="#playground">Try it below</a>
                <a class="btn" href="#docs">Read the docs</a>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="stage" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="editor">
                <div class="window-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>OrdersController.cs</span></div>
<pre><span style="color:var(--tok-key)">public class</span> <span style="color:var(--tok-type)">OrdersController</span>(<span style="color:var(--tok-type)">INotyfService</span> notyf) : <span style="color:var(--tok-type)">Controller</span>
{
    [<span style="color:var(--tok-type)">HttpPost</span>]
    <span style="color:var(--tok-key)">public</span> <span style="color:var(--tok-type)">IActionResult</span> <span style="color:var(--tok-type)">Create</span>(<span style="color:var(--tok-type)">Order</span> order)
    {
        <span style="color:var(--tok-com)">// ... save the order</span>
        <span id="hero-line" data-service="notyf"></span><span class="caret"></span>
        <span style="color:var(--tok-key)">return</span> RedirectToAction(<span style="color:var(--tok-str)">"Index"</span>);
    }
}</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="browser">
                <div class="window-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><div class="url">localhost:5000/orders</div></div>
                <div class="skeleton">
                    <div></div><div></div><div></div>
                    <div class="row"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="mock-toasts" id="mock-toasts"></div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ---------------------------------------------------------------- WHAT YOU GET *@
    <section class="container section is-shaded" id="features">
        <div class="section-head">
            <h2>What you get</h2>
            <p>Here's what v2 does out of the box.</p>
        </div>
        <ul class="facts">
            <li><strong>Toasts survive redirects</strong>They're kept in TempData, so they still show up after <code>RedirectToAction</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>fetch, jQuery and htmx just work</strong>Call an endpoint from JavaScript and the toast comes back with the response. There's nothing to wire up.</li>
            <li><strong>No jQuery</strong>v1 needed jQuery on the page. v2 is plain JavaScript, so you can drop it anywhere before <code>&lt;/body&gt;</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Works with a strict CSP</strong>There are no inline scripts, so <code>script-src 'self'</code> is fine. Using nonces? You can pass one in.</li>
            <li><strong>MVC, Razor Pages and Minimal APIs</strong>Inject <code>INotyfService</code> or <code>IToastifyService</code> wherever you handle the request.</li>
            <li><strong>Easy to style</strong>Set colours, icons and CSS classes per type, pick one of eight positions, or switch to right-to-left.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    @* ---------------------------------------------------------------- PLAYGROUND *@
    <section class="container section" id="playground">
        <div class="section-head">
            <h2>Try it right here</h2>
            <p>Pick a type, write a message and hit Fire. This page's server raises the toast with the same C# you see on the right.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel playground" id="pg-root">
            <div class="controls" id="pg-controls">
                <div class="field">
                    <span class="label">Library</span>
                    <div class="seg" data-pg="library">
                        <button type="button" data-value="notyf" aria-pressed="true">Notyf</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="toastify" aria-pressed="false">Toastify</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="field">
                    <span class="label">Type</span>
                    <div class="seg" data-pg="type">
                        <button type="button" data-value="Success" aria-pressed="true">Success</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="Error" aria-pressed="false">Error</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="Warning" aria-pressed="false">Warning</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="Information" aria-pressed="false">Info</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="Custom" aria-pressed="false">Custom</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="field">
                    <label for="pg-message">Message</label>
                    <input class="input" id="pg-message" type="text" value="Order placed!" maxlength="120" autocomplete="off" />
                </div>
                <div class="field">
                    <span class="label">Duration</span>
                    <div class="seg" data-pg="duration">
                        <button type="button" data-value="" aria-pressed="true">Default</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="2" aria-pressed="false">2s</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="10" aria-pressed="false">10s</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="0" aria-pressed="false">Sticky</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="field is-hidden" id="pg-color-field">
                    <span class="label">Colour</span>
                    <div class="swatches" data-pg="color">
                        <button type="button" data-value="#4c33d8" aria-pressed="true" style="background:#4c33d8" aria-label="Purple"></button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="#e8a54b" aria-pressed="false" style="background:#e8a54b" aria-label="Saffron"></button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="#2b6cb8" aria-pressed="false" style="background:#2b6cb8" aria-label="Blue"></button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="#1a7a3a" aria-pressed="false" style="background:#1a7a3a" aria-label="Green"></button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="#1a1814" aria-pressed="false" style="background:#1a1814" aria-label="Ink"></button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="code-out">
                <div class="code-head"><span>OrdersController.cs</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="pg-code">copy</button></div>
                <pre><code id="pg-code" class="language-csharp"></code></pre>
                <div class="fire">
                    <p>The button calls <code>POST /demo/toast</code>. The server raises the toast and it comes back in a response header.</p>
                    <button class="btn btn-primary" id="pg-fire" type="button">Fire</button>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ---------------------------------------------------------------- COMPARE *@
    <section class="container section is-shaded" id="compare">
        <div class="section-head">
            <h2>Notyf or Toastify?</h2>
            <p>The package wraps two JavaScript libraries. The C# API is the same for both, so you can switch later with a one-line change. Try each one and pick the look you like.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="compare">
            <div class="panel lib">
                <div class="lib-head"><h3>Notyf</h3><code>INotyfService</code></div>
                <p>Comes with icons, a ripple effect and eight positions, including full width. It also announces each toast to screen readers.</p>
                <div class="lib-buttons">
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="notyf:Success"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--ok)"></span>Success</button>
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="notyf:Error"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--err)"></span>Error</button>
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="notyf:Warning"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--warn)"></span>Warning</button>
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="notyf:Information"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--info)"></span>Info</button>
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="notyf:Custom"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--accent)"></span>Custom</button>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="panel lib">
                <div class="lib-head"><h3>Toastify</h3><code>IToastifyService</code></div>
                <p>Flat and minimal. It takes any CSS background, gradients included, and sits at the top or bottom, on the left or right.</p>
                <div class="lib-buttons">
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="toastify:Success"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--ok)"></span>Success</button>
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="toastify:Error"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--err)"></span>Error</button>
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="toastify:Warning"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--warn)"></span>Warning</button>
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="toastify:Information"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--info)"></span>Info</button>
                    <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="toastify:Custom"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--accent)"></span>Custom</button>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ---------------------------------------------------------------- SETUP WIZARD *@
    <section class="container section" id="setup">
        <div class="section-head">
            <h2>Set it up in your project</h2>
            <p>Pick your project type and library to get the exact code. It takes four steps.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="wizard">
            <div class="wizard-bar">
                <div class="seg" data-wizard="workload">
                    <button type="button" data-value="mvc" aria-pressed="true">MVC</button>
                    <button type="button" data-value="pages" aria-pressed="false">Razor Pages</button>
                    <button type="button" data-value="minimal" aria-pressed="false">Minimal API</button>
                </div>
                <div class="seg" data-wizard="library">
                    <button type="button" data-value="notyf" aria-pressed="true">Notyf</button>
                    <button type="button" data-value="toastify" aria-pressed="false">Toastify</button>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="wizard-steps" id="wizard-steps"></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ---------------------------------------------------------------- DOCS *@
    <section class="container section is-shaded" id="docs">
        <div class="section-head">
            <h2>Docs</h2>
            <p>Everything else, one topic at a time. Each section has a button so you can see it working.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="docs">
            <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Docs">
                <p>On this page</p>
                <a href="#types">Notification types</a>
                <a href="#duration">Duration &amp; sticky</a>
                <a href="#redirects">Redirects &amp; forms</a>
                <a href="#ajax">fetch, AJAX &amp; htmx</a>
                <a href="#minimal-api">Minimal APIs</a>
                <a href="#razor-pages">Razor Pages</a>
                <a href="#customize">Customize</a>
                <a href="#security">Security &amp; CSP</a>
                <a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a>
            </nav>

            <div>
                <div class="docs-toggle">
                    <span>Show code for</span>
                    <div class="seg" data-docs-lib>
                        <button type="button" data-value="notyf" aria-pressed="true">Notyf</button>
                        <button type="button" data-value="toastify" aria-pressed="false">Toastify</button>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <article class="doc" id="types">
                    <h2>Notification types</h2>
                    <p>Four built-in styles plus <code>Custom</code> for your own colour (and, with Notyf, an icon).</p>
                    <partial name="_CodePair" model="Snippets.Types" />
                    <div class="try">
                        <span class="try-label">Try it:</span>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="@@:Success">Success</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="@@:Error">Error</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="@@:Warning">Warning</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="@@:Information">Information</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="@@:Custom">Custom</button>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="doc" id="duration">
                    <h2>Duration &amp; sticky toasts</h2>
                    <p>Pass a duration in seconds. Leave it out for the global default. Pass <code>0</code> and it stays until closed - sticky toasts always get a close button.</p>
                    <partial name="_CodePair" model="Snippets.Duration" />
                    <div class="try">
                        <span class="try-label">Try it:</span>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="@@:Success" data-duration="2" data-message="Gone in 2 seconds.">2 seconds</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="@@:Warning" data-message="Uses the global default.">Default</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fire="@@:Error" data-duration="0" data-message="Stays until you close it.">Sticky</button>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="doc" id="redirects">
                    <h2>Redirects &amp; form posts</h2>
                    <p>Toasts are stored in TempData, so Post-Redirect-Get just works. Returning the view straight from a POST works too.</p>
                    <partial name="_CodePair" model="Snippets.Redirect" />
                    <div class="try">
                        <span class="try-label">Try it:</span>
                        <form method="post" asp-controller="Demo" asp-action="Save">
                            <input type="hidden" name="library" value="notyf" data-lib-input />
                            <button class="btn btn-sm" type="submit">POST, then redirect</button>
                        </form>
                        <form method="post" asp-controller="Demo" asp-action="Validate">
                            <input type="hidden" name="library" value="notyf" data-lib-input />
                            <button class="btn btn-sm" type="submit">POST, return View()</button>
                        </form>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="doc" id="ajax">
                    <h2>fetch, AJAX &amp; htmx</h2>
                    <p>Raise a toast in any endpoint and call it from the browser. It comes back in a response header and shows up on its own - on success <em>and</em> error responses. Nothing to wire up.</p>
                    <partial name="_Code" model="Snippets.Fetch" />
                    <partial name="_Code" model="Snippets.Htmx" />
                    <div class="try">
                        <span class="try-label">Try it:</span>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fetch="/demo/archive?client=fetch">fetch()</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-jquery="/demo/archive?client=jQuery">jQuery</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" id="htmx-demo" hx-post="/demo/htmx" hx-target="#htmx-out">htmx</button>
                        <button class="btn btn-sm" type="button" data-fetch="/demo/fail">HTTP 400</button>
                        <span id="htmx-out" class="htmx-out"></span>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="doc" id="minimal-api">
                    <h2>Minimal APIs</h2>
                    <p>Inject the service straight into the handler. Every playground button on this page goes through an endpoint like this one.</p>
                    <partial name="_CodePair" model="Snippets.MinimalApi" />
                </article>

                <article class="doc" id="razor-pages">
                    <h2>Razor Pages</h2>
                    <p>Inject the service into your page model and call it from a handler. <code>Page()</code> and <code>RedirectToPage()</code> both work. This app has a real one - open it and submit the form.</p>
                    <partial name="_Code" model="Snippets.CheckoutPage" />
                    <div class="try">
                        <span class="try-label">Try it:</span>
                        <a class="btn btn-sm" href="/checkout">Open the Razor Pages example →</a>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="doc" id="customize">
                    <h2>Customize</h2>
                    <p>Colours, CSS classes and placement - per type or for every toast.</p>
                    <partial name="_CodePair" model="Snippets.Customize" />
                    <div data-lib="notyf">
                        <div class="table-scroll"><table>
                            <thead><tr><th>SETTING</th><th>DEFAULT</th><th>WHAT IT DOES</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>DurationInSeconds</code></td><td>5</td><td>Default duration for every toast.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Position</code></td><td><code>BottomRight</code></td><td>Top/Bottom + Left/Center/Right, or <code>TopFullWidth</code> / <code>BottomFullWidth</code>.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>IsDismissable</code></td><td>false</td><td>Close button on every toast.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>HasRippleEffect</code></td><td>true</td><td>Notyf's ripple animation.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>IsRtl</code></td><td>false</td><td>Right-to-left layout.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>ClassName</code></td><td>-</td><td>Extra CSS class on every toast.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Success</code> … <code>Custom</code></td><td>v1 colours</td><td><code>BackgroundColor</code>, <code>ClassName</code>, <code>IconClassName</code> per type.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>IncludeFontAwesome</code></td><td>true</td><td>Loads Font Awesome 4.7 for the icons. Turn off if you bring your own.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>AutoHandleAjax</code></td><td>true</td><td>Shows toasts from fetch / XHR / htmx responses automatically.</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table></div>
                    </div>
                    <div data-lib="toastify" class="is-hidden">
                        <div class="table-scroll"><table>
                            <thead><tr><th>SETTING</th><th>DEFAULT</th><th>WHAT IT DOES</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>DurationInSeconds</code></td><td>5</td><td>Default duration for every toast.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Gravity</code></td><td><code>Bottom</code></td><td><code>Top</code> or <code>Bottom</code>.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Position</code></td><td><code>Right</code></td><td><code>Left</code> or <code>Right</code>.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>IsDismissable</code></td><td>false</td><td>Close button on every toast.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>ClassName</code></td><td>-</td><td>Extra CSS class on every toast.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>Success</code> … <code>Custom</code></td><td>v1 colours</td><td><code>BackgroundColor</code> and <code>ClassName</code> per type.</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>AutoHandleAjax</code></td><td>true</td><td>Shows toasts from fetch / XHR / htmx responses automatically.</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table></div>
                    </div>
                </article>

                <article class="doc" id="security">
                    <h2>Security &amp; CSP</h2>
                    <p>No inline JavaScript - toasts travel in a JSON data block, so <code>script-src 'self'</code> works out of the box. Using nonces? Pass yours in.</p>
                    <partial name="_CodePair" model="Snippets.Csp" />
                    <div class="callout"><span>⚠</span><span><strong>Messages are HTML.</strong> That's what lets you use <code>&lt;b&gt;</code> in a toast - and why user input must be encoded first.</span></div>
                    <partial name="_CodePair" model="Snippets.Encode" />
                </article>

                <article class="doc" id="troubleshooting">
                    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
                    <p>The handful of things that trip people up.</p>
                    <div class="table-scroll"><table>
                        <thead><tr><th>SYMPTOM</th><th>CAUSE</th><th>FIX</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>404 on <code>/_content/AspNetCoreHero.ToastNotification/…</code></td><td>Unpublished app running outside Development - static web assets are off.</td><td>Publish the app, or call <code>builder.WebHost.UseStaticWebAssets()</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>No toast after a fetch call</td><td><code>app.UseNotyf()</code> / <code>app.UseToastify()</code> is missing, or the call goes to another origin.</td><td>Add the middleware. Toasts from other origins are ignored on purpose.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Toast shows twice after upgrading</td><td>Old code still calls <code>getResponseHeaders(xhr)</code>.</td><td>Delete the call - v2 handles AJAX on its own.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Razor Pages POST returns 400</td><td>Razor Pages validates the antiforgery token on every POST.</td><td>Send the <code>RequestVerificationToken</code> header with fetch, or use a Minimal API endpoint.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Notyf Warning / Info icons missing</td><td><code>IncludeFontAwesome = false</code> without your own icon font.</td><td>Load Font Awesome, or set <code>IconClassName</code> per type.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table></div>
                    <partial name="_Code" model="Snippets.StaticAssets" />
                </article>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cta">
            <h2>Install it and try it in your own app</h2>
            <p>It's one NuGet package and three lines of setup. If you run into a problem or have an idea, open an issue on GitHub. Happy Coding :)</p>
            <div class="hero-cta">
                <div class="install">
                    <span class="prompt">$</span>
                    <code id="code-cta-install">dotnet add package AspNetCoreHero.ToastNotification</code>
                    <button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="code-cta-install">copy</button>
                </div>
                <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/codewithmukesh/ToastNotification">Open GitHub</a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</main>
